1
00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:03.003
In this lab, you'll apply your experience

2
00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:06.473
parsing and extracting information from documents for RAG.

3
00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.475
By the end of the lab, you'll have implemented

4
00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.478
a workflow for answering questions about a document.

5
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:15.348
We'll use some of the same libraries from the previous lessons,

6
00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.751
including OpenAI, LangChain, and Pillow.

7
00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.253
We'll use two models from OpenAI.

8
00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:23.056
text-embedding-3-small for our embedding model

9
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.625
to convert parsed chunks into vectors.

10
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.228
and gpt-4o-mini for our language model

11
00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:30.430
to provide answers to questions.

12
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.533
We'll use ChromaDB for vector database

13
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.936
and LangChain to connect retrieved chunks from the database

14
00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.938
to our prompts for the language model.

15
00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.674
We've also provided a utility to visualize

16
00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.611
chunks that were used to generate the answer.

17
00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.312
Let's take a look at the

18
00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:47.914
document we're working on in this lab.

19
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:50.116
It's the Apple's 10K report,

20
00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:53.720
a publicly available report that US companies must file

21
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.623
to regulatory agencies like SEC for audit.

22
00:00:56.723 --> 00:01:00.927
It provides detailed information about their business throughout the fiscal year.

23
00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:05.298
We've already run LandingAI ADE on the document for you.

24
00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:08.034
can find ADE parse results,

25
00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.737
.md and .json under ade_outputs

26
00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.239
in the lessons folder.

27
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.542
Remember from the previous lesson

28
00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.744
that parsing yields a markdown file

29
00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.946
containing all the data from the document

30
00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.549
along with a JSON file containing its metadata.

31
00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:24.951
Let's load the markdown content

32
00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.519
and display a preview for the

33
00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:29.322
first page. As you've seen in the previous lab,

34
00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.925
the markdown file contains snippets of HTML

35
00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:36.429
called anchor tag, which indicate the unique identifier of each chunk.

36
00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.331
We can use the ID to

37
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.300
link each chunk in the markdown file

38
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.602
to its metadata in the JSON file.

39
00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.671
Now let's load the chunks and

40
00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.806
examine the structure of the first chunk.

41
00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.476
Each chunk has five key fields.

42
00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.879
chunk_id, which is the unique identifier.

43
00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:55.582
chunk_type, what kind of content this is,

44
00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.384
text, table, figure, etc.

45
00:01:58.485 --> 00:02:00.687
The text, which is the actual content

46
00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.889
including the HTML anchor tags

47
00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.091
with unique ID for that chunk.

48
00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:08.061
bbox, bounding box coordinates for the chunk,

49
00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.064
page, which page this chunk came from.

50
00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.066
The bounding box is critical.

51
00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:15.135
This is what makes LandingAI different.

52
00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:19.038
Every chunk traces back to its exact location in the original document.

53
00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.241
When we retrieve a chunk later,

54
00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:23.042
we can show where it came from.

55
00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.311
Let's set up our vector database.

56
00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:27.714
As you can see here, we've already defined

57
00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.716
where we want to store our data.

58
00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.719
This path points to a folder called chroma_db

59
00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.721
right here in our lessons directory.

60
00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:38.024
And we're naming our collection ade_documents.

61
00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:41.227
This is where we'll store all our document embeddings.

62
00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.697
Next, we specify which embedding model to use.

63
00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:48.501
Here, we're using OpenAI's text-embedding-3-small model

64
00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:50.803
to convert text into embeddings.

65
00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.907
Now, this line instantiates our ChromaDB client.

66
00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:56.709
Notice we're using PersistentClient here,

67
00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.311
which is important. This means our

68
00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:00.213
data will be saved to our disk.

69
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:02.715
Finally, this line creates our collection.

70
00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:05.185
We're using get_or_create_collection,

71
00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:06.786
which is really convenient.

72
00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:09.589
If this collection already exists, it will load it.

73
00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.591
if not, it creates a new one.

74
00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.760
And that's the setup. We're now ready

75
00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.895
to start populating the database with chunks.

76
00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:19.098
We've already loaded the chunks into the vector database for you.

77
00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:21.301
So if you check for existing chunks,

78
00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:25.905
you'll see that there are already 453 existing in the collection.

79
00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.774
But let's say this is your first

80
00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.909
time loading ADE chunks into the vector database.

81
00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.111
Let's walk through the code to see how.

82
00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.914
We first loop through all our loaded chunks.

83
00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:38.117
For each chunk, we grab its unique chunk_id,

84
00:03:38.418 --> 00:03:41.120
then check if it already exists in our collection.

85
00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:45.291
Next, we extract the text and skip any empty chunks,

86
00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.895
and then generate embeddings using OpenAI's API.

87
00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.897
Now to prepare the metadata,

88
00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:54.400
we extract the chunk type, page number, and the bounding boxes.

89
00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:57.303
Once we add everything to our Chroma collection,

90
00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.706
you have the text, the chunk_id,

91
00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:02.108
metadata, and the embedding

92
00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.310
we just generated in the collection.

93
00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.212
As you can see, since the

94
00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:08.014
chunks were already pre-loaded, we added zero

95
00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:10.483
new chunks to the collection.

96
00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.486
Now that we have data and metadata in our database,

97
00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:16.289
we can query the vectors to retrieve relevant chunks.

98
00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.492
We'll write a function called rag_query to help us with this.

99
00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:21.995
The function takes four parameters.

100
00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.698
The question, the user's input question in natural language,

101
00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.968
top_k, how many results to return from the vector search,

102
00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:31.271
default is equal to three.

103
00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:35.275
The threshold, minimum similarity score to include in the results.

104
00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:39.045
default is 0.25 and show_images,

105
00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:42.649
whether to show visual grounding from the original PDF.

106
00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:46.419
default equals True. Let's break down what the function does.

107
00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.121
First, we embed the question,

108
00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.923
which converts your question to a vector

109
00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.326
the same embedding model we use for the chunks.

110
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:55.995
Second, we query ChromaDB to find the

111
00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:58.531
top K nearest vectors to your question.

112
00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:02.635
Third, parse results to extract documents, metadatas,

113
00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.738
distances, and IDs from the response.

114
00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:08.741
Fourth, we filter by the threshold.

115
00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:12.712
So we only show results above the minimum similarity score.

116
00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:16.215
And fifth, we visualize using our helper utilities,

117
00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:19.419
we extract and highlight the chunk from the original PDF

118
00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:22.221
to show exactly where the information came from.

119
00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.590
Let's test our query function.

120
00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:27.493
We'll ask about Apple's net sales in 2023.

121
00:05:27.694 --> 00:05:30.396
The same question we tried with regex earlier.

122
00:05:30.697 --> 00:05:32.799
Notice how we're saying top_k=5

123
00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.002
to get more results and threshold=0.32

124
00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:38.004
to filter out weaker matches.

125
00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.006
Let's look at the five results.

126
00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:42.575
The question was, what was Apple's net sales

127
00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.811
in 2023.

128
00:05:44.811 --> 00:05:46.946
Keep in mind, these are semantically similar

129
00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:49.115
answers, so they may not be perfect.

130
00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:52.485
The first response gives you a clear breakdown of net sales

131
00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:56.389
for the previous three years by products and services.

132
00:05:56.589 --> 00:06:00.493
Second, third, and fifth are giving you tangential information

133
00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:02.595
about net sales in 2023

134
00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.098
which doesn't directly answer the question.

135
00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:08.301
The fourth result provides the same answer as the first one.

136
00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.470
So in production, you can tweak the

137
00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.705
parameters to get the answers you actually need.

138
00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.308
What if you want to ask questions about a table?

139
00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:17.110
It is common that financial data

140
00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.011
is found in a lot of tables.

141
00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:21.214
This is where hybrid search can help us.

142
00:06:21.314 --> 00:06:25.618
First, we vectorize the question or the user input. What was

143
00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:28.020
Apple's total revenue in 2023.

144
00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.124
We'll still use semantic similarity, so we'll match total revenue

145
00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.126
to net sales by meaning.

146
00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:35.728
Second, we'll use metadata for filtering.

147
00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:38.431
So in this case, we're only looking at chunks

148
00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:40.800
where the chunk type is table.

149
00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.102
As you can see from the result,

150
00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:45.405
you can see the table tag in the markdown.

151
00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:48.107
which means that we're pulling information from tables.

152
00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:52.011
Feel free to use the helper methods to visualize this chunk.

153
00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:54.614
And feel free to use other

154
00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:57.016
filters like page number and chunk types.

155
00:06:57.316 --> 00:06:59.519
Now that we can retrieve relevant chunks,

156
00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:02.722
we can incorporate this into a full RAG pipeline.

157
00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:06.526
We can create a retriever object from our ChromaDB database,

158
00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:10.696
which slots into a LangChain chain. Then, we can build the

159
00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:14.100
actual RAG chain. And we need three components.

160
00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.503
First is the prompt template. We define a system prompt

161
00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:21.808
that instructs the LLM to use the retrieved context to answer questions.

162
00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:26.813
The context placeholder is where LangChain will inject the retrieved chunks,

163
00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.482
and the input is where the user's question goes.

164
00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:32.852
Second is the language model. We will initialize

165
00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:34.954
the LLM, gpt-5-mini,

166
00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:38.658
that will generate answers based on the retrieved context.

167
00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:42.962
Third is the RAG chain. We use LangChain's create_retrieval_chain

168
00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.865
to connect the retriever to our prompt and LLM.

169
00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.367
This chain automatically handles the flow.

170
00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:51.671
Take the user's question, retrieve relevant chunks,

171
00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:54.674
then inject them into the prompt to generate an answer.

172
00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:59.045
We could answer that question by inspecting the retrieved chunks directly.

173
00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:02.849
But what about questions that require comparing multiple chunks of information?

174
00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:06.519
For example, comparing total revenue across multiple years.

175
00:08:06.519 --> 00:08:11.519
How did total revenue trend between 2022 and 2023 for iPhone sales?

176
00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:13.826
This is where the language model can

177
00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.828
reason through multiple chunks of retrieved data.

178
00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:19.131
So beyond simple lookups, you can also use this approach to

179
00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:22.134
look for discrepancies across multiple documents.

180
00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:25.338
Get the latest version of an amendment in a legal document.

181
00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:27.640
List out all versions of that amendment

182
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.143
or get a summary on a specific topic with grounded sources.

183
00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.946
You can do things like analyzing research papers,

184
00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:38.651
reports, and regulatory documents for commercializing a drug.

185
00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:43.155
In summary, by combining embeddings, vector databases and

186
00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:47.059
RAG, you can transform raw extracted data into accurate insights

187
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.762
from multiple documents and files,

188
00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:53.065
delivering faster, smarter document understanding.

189
00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:54.934
In our next lesson, we'll review

190
00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.869
how to deploy ADE in the cloud,

191
00:08:57.069 --> 00:09:00.273
scaling our RAG application through an event-driven architecture

192
00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:04.076
that automatically triggers ADE for document processing

193
00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:07.246
whenever new documents appear. I'll see you there.
